PLACES=[
N('portland-observatory-me','Portland Observatory','Portland','mes','landmark','standing',43.66538,-70.24825,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1807',
  'An 86-foot octagonal signal tower put up on Munjoy Hill in 1807, organised by Captain Lemuel Moody, so that ships could be seen and signalled with flags hours before they reached the wharves. It served as a watch tower in the War of 1812. It is the only known surviving tower of its kind in the United States and became a National Historic Landmark in 2006.',
  'A seven-storey shingled tower on a base of heavy fieldstone that serves as ballast; there is no basement. The last major restoration kept as much original wood as it could. Inside, guided tours of about 45 minutes climb to the top, ten people at a time.',
  'The last maritime signal tower in the country, still on its hill above the harbour.',
  'Greater Portland Landmarks runs it as a museum, 138 Congress Street. Tours first come, first served; no advance tickets. Closed from mid-October to late May. Street parking on Munjoy Hill. Season and times from the Landmarks page, which still gave the 2025 dates when read.',
  [W('Portland Observatory'),['Greater Portland Landmarks: Portland Observatory',GPL],NR('73000122')],
  brick='no',hours='Late May to mid-October (2025: May 24 to October 13), tours 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., last entry 3:30; some summer evenings 4:30 to 7:30'),
N('fort-gorges','Fort Gorges','Portland','mes','fort','ruins',43.66306,-70.22139,'site','boat','boat','forts',
  '1858–1865',
  'A granite fort on Hog Island Ledge in Casco Bay, funded in 1857 and built from 1858, with work speeded by the Civil War, and finished in 1865 as the war ended. Designed by Colonel Reuben Staples Smart, it is about the size of Fort Sumter but built of granite. Iron-clad ships and long-range guns made it out of date before it was ever used: no battle was fought there and no garrison was stationed there.',
  'An abandoned fort on its own ledge: casemates, powder magazines and a parade ground, overgrown in places. A 300-pounder Parrott rifle is still in place, one of the largest Civil War guns left. Wikipedia suggests a flashlight for the magazines. Condition and safety on the island not checked.',
  'A granite fort finished the year the war ended and never once used.',
  'A City of Portland park reached only by private boat, kayak or hired water taxi; there is no ferry. The old landing is badly storm-damaged and the island has no toilets or water. Friends of Fort Gorges arranges boat tours and private visits on request and warns that the ground is rough and uneven. The city\'s own page could not be opened, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Fort Gorges'),['Friends of Fort Gorges','https://www.friendsoffortgorges.org/'],NR('73000114')],
  brick='?'),
N('portland-city-hall-me','Portland City Hall','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65925,-70.25726,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1909–1912',
  'The fourth city hall built in Portland, on a rise at the head of Exchange Street. The one before it, built in 1867 after the Great Fire, burned in 1908. This granite building, inspired by New York City Hall, went up from 1909 to 1912. Its auditorium holds the Kotzschmar Memorial Organ, the second-largest organ in the world when it was built in 1912.',
  'A U-shaped granite building open to Congress Street, with a 200-foot central tower, round-arched ground-floor windows and Tuscan colonnades on the wings. A modern addition on Myrtle Street holds the city offices\' overflow and Merrill Auditorium, where the organ is. Interior not checked.',
  'The city hall that replaced the one that burned in 1908, with a famous organ inside.',
  '389 Congress Street. The city\'s offices are inside; the City of Portland\'s page could not be opened when this was written, so office hours are not checked. Metered street parking nearby.',
  [W('Portland City Hall (Maine)'),NR('73000119')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('us-custom-house-portland-me','United States Custom House','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65731,-70.25130,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1867–1872',
  'The Great Fire of July 4, 1866 burned the Exchange Building, which had held the customs office, post office and courts. Alfred B. Mullett, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, designed this replacement, built from 1867 to 1872 of New Hampshire granite with a slate roof, materials chosen to resist fire. Getting granite for the upper storeys slowed the work.',
  'A three-storey granite building in a mix of Second Empire and Renaissance Revival, with round-headed windows between Doric columns. Wikipedia describes the two-storey customs hall inside: a checkerboard marble floor, long counters of several marbles quarried on an island in Lake Champlain, and walnut woodwork intact through the building. Whether the public may go in was not checked.',
  'A fireproof granite custom house built straight after the 1866 fire.',
  '312 Fore Street, near the waterfront. A federal building: look from the street. Metered street parking and city garages nearby.',
  [W('United States Custom House (Portland, Maine)'),NR('73000128')],
  brick='no'),
N('gignoux-courthouse-portland','Edward T. Gignoux U.S. Courthouse','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65889,-70.25448,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1908–1911, completed 1932',
  'The first federal courthouse in Maine, designed by James Knox Taylor, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, in the Italian Renaissance Revival style. Begun in 1908 and opened in 1911 as a U, it was closed into a full ring with an interior courtyard in 1931 and 1932. It stands by Lincoln Park with the Custom House, the county courthouse and City Hall.',
  'Faced entirely with New England granite: two storeys over a raised stone base, a slate mansard roof with round dormers, and buff brick walls in the courtyard. Inside, Wikipedia describes a rotunda with a curving marble staircase and a restored courtroom; it is a working courthouse and visiting was not checked.',
  'Maine\'s first federal courthouse, granite outside and marble within.',
  '156 Federal Street. A working federal court: look from the street. Metered street parking nearby.',
  [W('Edward T. Gignoux United States Courthouse'),NR('74000168')],
  brick='no',bcol='buff'),
N('first-parish-church-portland-me','First Parish Church','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.65855,-70.25824,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1825',
  'Built in 1825 of Freeport granite for a congregation founded in 1674, on the site of the 1740 "Old Jerusalem" meeting house. It is the oldest church building in Portland and was the only one in the area to come through the Great Fire of 1866. Hermann Kotzschmar was its organist for 47 years. The congregation is Unitarian Universalist.',
  'A Federal-style granite church with a square tower, a clock, an octagonal belfry and a short spire. It kept the clock and weathervane of the church before it. Inside, a chandelier holds a 12-pound cannonball fired at the old meeting house when the British attacked the town in 1775. Interior not checked beyond that.',
  'The church that came through the 1866 fire, with a 1775 cannonball in its chandelier.',
  '425 Congress Street, near City Hall. The congregation\'s own page could not be opened, so service times are not given here. Metered street parking nearby.',
  [W('First Parish Church (Portland, Maine)'),NR('73000113')],
  brick='no'),
N('cathedral-immaculate-conception-portland-me','Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.66175,-70.25437,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1866–1869',
  'The seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Portland, a Gothic Revival church designed by the New York architect Patrick Keely. Work began in 1866, was interrupted by the Great Fire that year, and was finished on September 8, 1869. The cathedral was restored in 1921, 1969 and 2000, and its tallest steeple, at 204 feet, made it the tallest building in Portland until 2023.',
  'Red brick with sandstone trim and a slate roof; a sandstone Gothic arch over the main door with a stained-glass rose window above, and a buttressed tower with an octagonal spire. The parish hall runs off the back and the bishop\'s residence faces Congress Street. Interior not checked.',
  'A red-brick Gothic cathedral begun in the year of the Great Fire.',
  'Diocese of Portland, 307 Congress Street (the main front is on Cumberland Avenue). The diocese\'s page refused to open; the Mass times here are as a web search quoted the diocese\'s own listing in November 2026, and may change for funerals. Metered street parking nearby.',
  [W('Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception (Portland, Maine)'),NR('85001257')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='Masses Saturday 4 p.m.; Sunday 7, 8 and 10 a.m., and noon in Latin'),
N('victoria-mansion-portland','Victoria Mansion (Morse-Libby House)','Portland','mes','landmark','standing',43.65154,-70.26075,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1858–1860',
  'A brownstone Italianate villa finished in 1860 as a summer home for the hotel man Ruggles Sylvester Morse, designed by Henry Austin of New Haven. The Libby family later owned it and gave its contents. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1971 as a particularly well-preserved Italianate house.',
  'A four-storey tower, deep eaves, verandas and ornate windows. Inside, Gustave Herter\'s only intact commission and the earliest known, wall paintings and trompe-l\'oeil by Giuseppe Guidicini, and about 97 percent of the original contents: furniture, carpets, gas fixtures, stained glass, porcelain and silver. Shown by guided tour in the regular season and self-paced in the holiday season.',
  'A brownstone villa with almost everything still inside it from 1860.',
  'A house museum, 109 Danforth Street. Timed tickets; the museum suggests booking ahead. Closed Mondays in the holiday season and on some holidays. On-street parking on Danforth, High, State, Park and Pleasant Streets. Hours from the museum\'s own page.',
  [W('Victoria Mansion'),['Victoria Mansion: Visit',VM],NR('70000074')],
  brick='no',hours='May 1 to October 31, daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; day after Thanksgiving to the Sunday after New Year\'s, Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('porteous-mitchell-braun-portland','Porteous, Mitchell and Braun Company Building','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65578,-70.26101,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1904, enlarged 1911',
  'Built in 1904 and enlarged in 1911 for Porteous, Mitchell and Braun, for many years the largest department store in Maine. It is a Renaissance Revival commercial block, listed on the National Register in 1996, and now the main building of the Maine College of Art and Design.',
  'Five storeys on a steel frame, the brick walls clad in pale terra cotta: fluted pilasters, three-part windows with slender columns, round-arched windows on the top floor and a corbelled cornice. The ground floor is entirely modern glass and stone. A college building: look from Congress Street.',
  'A terra-cotta department store from 1904, now an art college.',
  '522 to 528 Congress Street, in the Arts District. Maine College of Art and Design: look from the street. Metered street parking.',
  [W('Porteous, Mitchell and Braun Company Building'),NR('96001039')],
  brick='no'),
N('woodman-building-portland','Woodman Building','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65871,-70.25333,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1867',
  'A four-storey commercial block put up in 1867, the year after the Great Fire, paid for by the Woodman family and designed by George M. Harding. The Register form calls it the most high-style commercial building raised after the fire and the grandest Second Empire building of its kind in Maine. Its ground-floor iron was cast from Harding\'s plans by the Portland Company.',
  'Brick on a granite foundation, with cast-iron pilasters and arches on the ground floor (an iron plate under the left pilaster names Harding as architect), carved stone arches over the upper windows, a plaque reading "A.D. 1867 Woodman", and a slate mansard roof with big corner dormers. The form (1972) found it virtually unchanged, the roof slate original. Present use not checked.',
  'The grandest of the blocks built in the year after the Great Fire.',
  '133 to 141 Middle Street, on the corner of Pearl Street. Private: look from the street. Metered street parking.',
  [NR('72000075'),['National Register nomination (1972)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/1cc7359e-b840-405a-93ed-fa73687655db']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('rackleff-building-portland','Rackleff Building','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.65873,-70.25302,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1867',
  'A three-storey Second Empire block built in 1867 to designs by George M. Harding, who also designed the Woodman Block next door and the Thompson Block beyond; the Woodman and Rackleff buildings fill a whole city block between them. Its rounded corner wraps onto Church Street.',
  'Brick with freestone trim. The cast-iron arcade on the ground floor matches the Woodman Block\'s but sits about a foot lower. A plaque reads "Rackleff"; the wooden cornice is carried on paired freestone brackets with bands of carved quatrefoils. A shield with an "H", likely for Harding, is on the Church Street side. Present use not checked.',
  'A curved-corner block of 1867, twin to the Woodman next door.',
  '127 to 133 Middle Street, at Church Street. Private: look from the street. Metered street parking.',
  [NR('73000123'),['National Register nomination (1973)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/1a4b8ebb-11c4-4532-80ea-d3dd5ead3915']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red'),
N('williston-west-church-portland','Williston-West Church and Parish House','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.64805,-70.27087,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1877 or 1897; parish house 1905',
  'A Victorian Gothic church by Francis H. Fassett, with a parish house added in 1905 by John Calvin Stevens and John Howard Stevens. The Christian Endeavor movement, a forerunner of modern youth ministry, was founded here in 1881. Sources disagree on the church\'s date: the Register form gives 1877, Wikipedia and the Register record 1897. The congregation merged with another in 2011 and the property was sold for redevelopment.',
  'A large brick complex with a square three-storey corner tower, buttressed walls, Gothic windows and rose windows; the form notes that Stevens stripped some of the High Victorian ornament from the tower in 1905. Wikipedia says it now houses professional offices. Interior not checked.',
  'Where the Christian Endeavor youth movement began in 1881.',
  '32 Thomas Street, at Carroll Street, in the West End. Private offices: look from the street. On-street parking.',
  [W('Williston-West Church and Parish House'),NR('80000232'),['National Register nomination (1980)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/3a9839c6-68bd-4cad-944d-3223025e67d1']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('state-street-church-portland-me','State Street Congregational Church','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.65264,-70.26537,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1851',
  'A Congregational church designed by William Washburn and built in 1851, west of the ground the Great Fire of 1866 burned. Also called the State Street Church and State Street United Church of Christ. Since about 2023 the congregation has also run a shelter for homeless families in the building.',
  'The church\'s stonework and interior were not checked; the Sunday service is open to all.',
  'An 1851 church that still holds its Sunday service.',
  '159 State Street. Free street parking on State Street on Sundays, and on Sunday mornings the church says you may use the Cumberland Club lot (not on Mother\'s Day or Easter). Service time from the church\'s own page.',
  [W('State Street Congregational Church'),['State Street Church','https://www.statestreetchurch.org/']],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday service 10 a.m.'),
N('st-dominics-church-portland-me','St. Dominic\'s Church (Maine Irish Heritage Center)','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.65047,-70.26269,'exact','open','walk','',
  '1833',
  'Dedicated on August 11, 1833 as the first Roman Catholic church in Portland and the third in Maine, it served the Irish neighbourhoods of Gorham\'s Corner, Munjoy Hill and Knightville and was once one of the largest Irish-American parishes north of Boston. The diocese closed the parish in 1997 and merged it into Sacred Heart. In 2003 the building passed to the new Maine Irish Heritage Center.',
  'The church stands at the corner of Gray and State Streets and is now the heritage center, with a genealogy library and events. Interior not checked.',
  'Portland\'s first Catholic church, now home to its Irish heritage center.',
  'Maine Irish Heritage Center, 34 Gray Street. Its own pages are drawn by script and did not give opening hours when read; a web search quoted its site as giving library hours on Thursdays, 10 a.m. to 2 p.m., not confirmed. No parking of its own; street parking nearby.',
  [W('St. Dominic\'s Church (Portland, Maine)'),['Maine Irish Heritage Center','https://maineirish.com/']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('sacred-heart-church-portland-me','Sacred Heart Church','Portland','mes','worship','standing',43.65534,-70.27013,'site','view','walk','',
  '1896–1915',
  'A Renaissance Revival church modelled on Notre-Dame de la Garde in Marseille, designed by Francis H. Fassett and Edward F. Fassett; it was Francis Fassett\'s last commission. Built from 1896 to 1915 for the city\'s third Catholic parish, mostly Irish, it held its first Mass on April 4, 1897. Repairs were needed for years, and Wikipedia reports a last Mass on October 25, 2026, after repairs were put at six million dollars.',
  'A stone church with a bell tower in the Parkside neighbourhood. Its roof and stonework were repaired in 1999 to 2001. With the church closed, look from the street; what becomes of it was not known when this was written.',
  'A church modelled on a Marseille basilica, closed in 2026.',
  'Roman Catholic Diocese of Portland. Closed since its last Mass: look from the street. On-street parking.',
  [W('Sacred Heart Church (Portland, Maine)')],
  brick='?'),
N('fidelity-trust-building-portland','Fidelity Trust Building','Portland','mes','civic','standing',43.6574,-70.2594,'site','view','walk','',
  '1910',
  'Maine\'s first skyscraper, a ten-storey, 135-foot Beaux-Arts office block on Monument Square designed by George Henri Desmond of Boston. It opened in 1910 in place of the Portland Theatre, also known as Ward\'s Opera House and the Nickel Theatre. Its three elevators were made by the Portland Company on Munjoy Hill.',
  'An ornate facade wraps the side and top of the building. It has carried several bank names (Maine Bank and Trust, People\'s United, M&T). Interior not checked.',
  'Maine\'s first skyscraper, on the site of the old Nickel Theatre.',
  'Monument Square, Congress Street. An office building: look from the square. Metered street parking and city garages nearby.',
  [W('Fidelity Trust Building (Portland, Maine)')],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-preble-south-portland','Fort Preble','South Portland','mes','fort','ruins',43.64916,-70.22590,'site','public','walk','forts',
  '1808–1950',
  'Built in 1808 as a star fort of stone, brick and sod, with Fort Scammell on House Island, to guard Portland Harbor; named for Commodore Edward Preble. It was garrisoned through every war from 1812 to the Second World War. Granite casemates for a bigger fort were begun and abandoned unfinished. The army left in 1950 and in 1952 the State of Maine took it over for the school that is now Southern Maine Community College.',
  'Part of the unfinished granite casemates and the fort wall facing the harbour, and several early 20th-century brick buildings (officers\' quarters, barracks, a fire station) now used by the college. A 900-foot granite breakwater of 1951 runs from the fort to Spring Point Ledge Light. The stairs over the bunker were removed after storm damage and a stabilization project was finished in November 2023.',
  'A star fort of 1808 that served until 1950, now on a college campus.',
  'Southern Maine Community College, South Portland campus. The college keeps the Spring Point Shoreway Trail and Greenbelt Walkway open to the public across the fort ground; signs route walkers round the bunker by Bunker Lane to Fort Road, where you can see the fort wall and the lighthouse. Most campus lots are college lots: visitor parking was not checked.',
  [W('Fort Preble'),['Southern Maine Community College: Fort Preble and the Greenbelt','https://www.smccme.edu/about/fort-preble-the-greenbelt/']],
  brick='?'),
N('casco-castle-tower-freeport','Casco Castle tower','Freeport','mes','hotel','ruins',43.81877,-70.11173,'site','view','drive','burned,hotels,trolleyparks',
  '1903–1914',
  'Casco Castle was a resort hotel built in 1903 by Amos F. Gerald, manager of the Portland and Brunswick Street Railway, to bring riders to South Freeport by trolley; designed by William R. Miller to look like a castle. Visitors crossed a suspension bridge 70 feet above Spark Creek. It had rooms for about a hundred guests, a zoo and a ball field. The hotel burned in 1914; its stone tower was spared.',
  'The 185-foot stone tower is all that is left of the castle; the wooden hotel stood to its south.',
  'A trolley company\'s castle hotel burned in 1914; only its stone tower stands.',
  'The tower is on private property and closed to the public. Wikipedia says it can be seen from Harraseeket Road or from Winslow Memorial Park across the Harraseeket River to the south.',
  [W('Casco Castle')],
  brick='no'),
]
